WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.157
- Simpsons, check.
Family guy, you're next.

00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:02.990
(makes beeping sounds)

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.848
- Last weeks flu scare got me thinking,

00:00:04.848 --> 00:00:07.270
I don't know anything
about my genetic make up

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.407
or what other illnesses I
might be susceptible to.

00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:10.540
- You know what's funny about this?

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.240
There's actually a genetic test for dogs.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.060
I actually did it for Bear and Roxy,

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.330
turns out they are what
we thought they were.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.660
They are who we thought they were.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.650
- So it made me wanna do
a 23andMe genetic test.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.720
- I think the one I did was called Embark.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.100
- I'm predisposed to
light caffeine, so true.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.250
- That's such a problem with
these genetic tests, oh my God.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.150
Like so many of them
will give you information

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:33.670
that is genetically
accurate to some degree,

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.780
and then they tell you
something that you cannot

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.040
practically act on at all.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.170
You know, we have a
higher likelihood that you

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.674
may live five years less
than a normal person would.

00:00:42.674 --> 00:00:44.330
Okay. What do I do with that?

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.296
What use is it to know
that you are potentially

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.750
have a higher risk for
Alzheimer's disease?

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.010
It's just like flexing.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.390
The genetic people are like, 'Ah!"

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.740
- Phew, don't have the
Alzheimer's gene, that's good.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:55.787
- See, I knew it!

00:00:55.787 --> 00:00:57.740
- And it says I don't
have the Alzheimer's gene,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:58.573
so that's good.

00:00:58.573 --> 00:00:59.860
(laughs)

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.382
- How would you like to be a male nurse?

00:01:01.382 --> 00:01:04.330
- Do I get to wear those
green pajama clothes all day?

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.163
- Yes.

00:01:05.163 --> 00:01:06.230
- AKA Scrubs, they're not pajamas

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.250
although they are kind
of comfy like pajamas.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.473
- Today I need you to observe surgery.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:12.917
- Boo!

00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:14.133
(knocks on glass)

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:17.230
Boo! My sister can attach
ventricles better than you!

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.320
- Why are they attaching
ventricles of the heart?

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.520
I'm so confused what
surgery he was looking at.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.870
I could have sworn the abdomen
was open in that scene.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.480
There are operating theaters

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.010
where you can watch
operations be performed.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.640
It's great for residents,
fellows, even other doctors

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.580
that are trying to learn.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.320
I don't think they let
family members do that.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.580
I have seen it in Grey's Anatomy,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.150
but I've never seen it in real life,

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:36.983
it goes to show.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.763
That looks like a weird ECG pattern.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:43.333
Oh, flat line.

00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:44.500
You know what we do?!

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.160
Chest compressions! chest compressions!

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.633
chest compressions! code blue!

00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:49.610
(makes a whooping sound)

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:50.565
- Come on, you gotta live, goddammit.

00:01:50.565 --> 00:01:51.830
I need you.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.084
Okay. Those are not
good chest compressions.

00:01:55.084 --> 00:01:57.250
(punching sounds)

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.270
Okay. So ACLS, advanced
cardiac life support,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.020
would not tell you to punch
a chicken in the face.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.683
Just want to put that out for the record.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:07.890
- What are you doing Peter?

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.083
- I'm making my own Red Bull.

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:10.902
Lois can't stop me from experiencing

00:02:10.902 --> 00:02:13.620
the manic highs and lows my body demands.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.740
- Kerosene?!

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.000
Why is he putting Kerosene in it?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.450
Red Bull has a lot of stuff in it.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.990
Like 80 milligrams of caffeine, Taurine.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.990
If you want a caffeine boost,
just have a cup of coffee.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.325
It's more natural.

00:02:24.325 --> 00:02:26.310
There's actually polyphenols in there

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.470
because it's plant-based.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.410
Coffee beans are just
better for your health,

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.043
at least our research shows that.

00:02:31.043 --> 00:02:32.500
- That drink will kill you.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.449
- Brian, whatever kills
me makes me stronger.

00:02:34.449 --> 00:02:36.240
- I think he messed that one up.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.729
- See Brian, I feel great.

00:02:38.729 --> 00:02:40.840
- Peter? Peter, are you alive?

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.360
- Poison hotline stat!

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.250
By the way, did you know

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.860
that there's a nationwide poison hotline?

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.260
- All the toxic chemicals
your husband ingested

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.500
from his homemade energy drink

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.190
have caused total kidney failure.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.850
- Total kidney failure
requires dialysis and quickly.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.900
Where you essentially take
the blood outside the body,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.350
clean it and process it
the way the kidneys do,

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.690
and put it back into the body.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.300
- We'll put them on
kidney transplant list,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.530
but that could take months.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.810
Until a donor is found,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.590
he's gonna have to undergo
dialysis treatments

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:05.423
three times a week.

00:03:05.423 --> 00:03:08.181
- Dialysis? Is there any other way?

00:03:08.181 --> 00:03:09.520
- What about me?

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.430
- What's the difference
between a dog kidney

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.263
and a human kidney?

00:03:12.263 --> 00:03:13.556
I have no idea.

00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:16.570
I assume it's similar, but
I feel like dog kidney's

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.000
might not last as long.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.370
I don't know.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.360
Bear, I would never harvest your organs.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.810
I will not ever harvest your kidneys.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.710
They are yours, and
even if we are a match,

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.350
I will make sure you get to keep them.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.183
Why are you so messy?

00:03:30.183 --> 00:03:31.016
(Offline beeping sound)

00:03:31.016 --> 00:03:33.042
- Chances are Peter never would
have survived the surgery.

00:03:33.042 --> 00:03:34.875
I mean dog kidneys?

00:03:34.875 --> 00:03:37.186
I'm not even sure dogs have kidneys.

00:03:37.186 --> 00:03:38.644
The dogs have kidneys?

00:03:38.644 --> 00:03:39.690
- Yes.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.124
- Ah, this? This is the guy.

00:03:42.124 --> 00:03:44.581
(laughs and claps his hands)

00:03:44.581 --> 00:03:45.663
- That's what I felt like!

00:03:45.663 --> 00:03:48.105
- Maybe you put on a few pounds, sweetie.

00:03:48.105 --> 00:03:50.670
- Lois, I weigh the same 293 pounds today

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.110
that I weighed in grade school.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.326
- Perhaps it's this lump on
the side of your neck, Sir.

00:03:54.326 --> 00:03:55.159
- Oh!

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:55.992
- Oh my God, Peter--

00:03:55.992 --> 00:03:56.825
- That does not look safe.

00:03:56.825 --> 00:03:57.876
- Hoe long have you had that thing?

00:03:57.876 --> 00:03:59.270
- I don't know.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.230
- We got to go see Dr. Hartman right away.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.988
- Quick differential
diagnosis, Cushing syndrome,

00:04:03.988 --> 00:04:07.980
Lipoma, soft tissue mass like a sarcoma.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.260
Could it be a lymphoma?

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.420
And he has a swollen lymph node there?

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.100
I guess that's possible as well.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.337
- No, you're stupid! Oh, hello, Griffins.

00:04:15.337 --> 00:04:16.750
What can I help you with today?

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:17.670
- That should be a warning sign.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.040
If you walk into a doctor's office

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:20.955
and they're just yelling at
a patient, "You're stupid!"

00:04:20.955 --> 00:04:24.222
- Dr. Hartman, Peter has a
huge lump growing on his neck.

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:26.140
- Mrs. Griffin, that's called a head.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.144
I've been fooled by that one before.

00:04:27.144 --> 00:04:27.977
(laughs)

00:04:27.977 --> 00:04:28.923
- Will you take a look at it?

00:04:28.923 --> 00:04:30.700
I think it might be a tumor.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:31.880
- Okay. Let's have a look.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.330
It appears you have a
subcutaneous vestigial twin

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:36.210
growing on your neck.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.490
- Is he saying that it's a
Teratoma just by touching it?

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:41.180
I mean, that's very impressive

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.247
that he was able to diagnose it,

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:45.250
so much so that it is not real.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.300
- A Teratoma is basically a type of tumor

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.700
that has all sorts of
different stem cells in it.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.470
Where they start growing
different cell lines,

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.040
Like hair, teeth, muscle.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.350
You cut it open

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.140
and there's just like
almost a twin in there.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:56.973
It's crazy.

00:04:56.973 --> 00:04:58.210
You can have human remains, almost.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.500
- Now hold still, I'm just gonna give you

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.546
a little topical anesthetic
and we'll have a closer look.

00:05:01.546 --> 00:05:02.379
(Scoffs)

00:05:02.379 --> 00:05:03.440
- What?

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.326
Ow, that would not,

00:05:04.326 --> 00:05:07.310
you'd need injection anesthetic.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.466
A little Lidocaine.

00:05:08.466 --> 00:05:09.488
- Hi everybody!

00:05:09.488 --> 00:05:10.760
(laughs)

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:12.590
- He did a skin flap
though, that's impressive.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:13.873
- Get Up, we're late.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.750
- Oh, he does not look good.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.600
He looks like he has anemia,

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.223
potentially some dehydration there.

00:05:20.223 --> 00:05:22.270
Oh my God, Jaundice.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:23.720
There's a little yellowing of the eyes.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:24.910
- How did this happened?!

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:25.792
- It's from smoking.

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:26.625
- Oh!

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:27.458
- It's not that bad.

00:05:27.458 --> 00:05:28.950
- Peter, you can't live like this.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.303
I'm taking you to Dr. Hartman.

00:05:30.303 --> 00:05:31.946
- No, I hate the doctor.

00:05:31.946 --> 00:05:33.079
- Are you sure?

00:05:33.079 --> 00:05:35.430
There are Cheez-It's in there.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:36.263
- Cheez-It's?!

00:05:36.263 --> 00:05:37.096
(laughs)

00:05:37.096 --> 00:05:37.929
- It's like a vet visit.

00:05:37.929 --> 00:05:38.762
I'm not gonna lie,

00:05:38.762 --> 00:05:40.057
I have some patients that
come in and I'm like,

00:05:40.057 --> 00:05:41.640
"Oh, like when's the last
time you went to a doctor?"

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.490
They're like, you know, three, four years.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.160
Mostly because I hate
going to see the doctor.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.260
I hate doctors.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.093
And I'm like,

00:05:47.093 --> 00:05:48.854
(crickets chirping)

00:05:48.854 --> 00:05:49.687
(makes a popping sound)

00:05:49.687 --> 00:05:50.520
Hey.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:52.930
- Mr. Griffin, I'm gonna need
to ask you a few questions.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:54.781
Do you think I can jump and
touch that pipe up there?

00:05:54.781 --> 00:05:55.614
(laughs)

00:05:55.614 --> 00:05:57.060
- I don't know why, we
always used to do that a kid.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.587
Street signs, crosswalk signs,
scaffolding's was always,

00:06:00.587 --> 00:06:02.040
"Can you touch that? Do you
think you can touch that?

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:02.990
Five bucks if I can touch that."

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:04.530
- Dr. Hartman, we're here for you

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.816
to help Peter quit smoking.

00:06:05.816 --> 00:06:07.108
- Mrs Griffin, I can't do that.

00:06:07.108 --> 00:06:08.440
It's an addiction.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.610
- Well you gotta do something!

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.620
- Well we absolutely could
do something about that.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.797
Not only do we have behavioral
treatment options for it,

00:06:13.797 --> 00:06:15.900
but we have pharmaceutical options for it

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.380
including nicotine
patches, oral medications.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:19.752
All of them are not right for everybody.

00:06:19.752 --> 00:06:21.555
And you have to do an
individual discussion

00:06:21.555 --> 00:06:23.670
with your doctor to figure
out what's right for you.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.920
And the first step of quitting
is contemplating quitting.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.344
I guess even before that is understanding

00:06:28.344 --> 00:06:29.840
why quitting is important.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.848
- But Dr. Hartman, why
did he lose consciousness?

00:06:31.848 --> 00:06:32.906
- Well, keeping in mind,

00:06:32.906 --> 00:06:35.400
the recent behavioral
changes you've described

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.462
I did a CAT scan.

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:37.295
- On a dog?

00:06:37.295 --> 00:06:38.128
(Peter laughs)

00:06:38.128 --> 00:06:39.310
- Oh, that's actually pretty funny.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.320
A CAT scan on a dog.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.340
God, we're such nerds.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.830
- However, I'm sorry to inform you

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.900
that Brian has a brain tumor.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:45.733
- Oh no.

00:06:45.733 --> 00:06:46.566
(Family gasps)

00:06:46.566 --> 00:06:48.749
- So doc, how much Jamba
juice do I have to drink

00:06:48.749 --> 00:06:50.215
to cure this?

00:06:50.215 --> 00:06:53.340
I've also heard that crystals exist.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.120
- He's been reading a little
bit too much of goop.com.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.706
- Do you think the brain
tumor might explain

00:06:57.706 --> 00:06:59.975
why Brian's been acting so odd lately?

00:06:59.975 --> 00:07:03.510
- Hmm, I haven't heard of
brains being linked to behavior,

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.000
but I suppose anything is possible.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.590
Well, it says here Brian's tumor

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.135
is for office use only.

00:07:08.135 --> 00:07:10.493
- Ugh, benign, it says benign.

00:07:10.493 --> 00:07:13.810
- Tumors can either be described
as benign or malignant,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.500
malignant meaning that
they're problematic.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.280
They can spread, they can
actually cause you to die.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.100
Benign tumors, usually the only way

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.620
that they cause a problem is
if they can turn malignant

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.790
or if they become big enough in size

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:26.050
where they exhibit mass effect.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.235
Putting pressure on other structures,

00:07:27.235 --> 00:07:29.310
causing them to malfunction.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.930
- Is my father going to be okay?

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.150
- Is that House, M.D.?

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.285
- He's in a coma, Mrs Griffin.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:36.457
- Oh, they got Gregory
House for this, wow.

00:07:36.457 --> 00:07:38.516
- Dr. House, if you're
gonna save this patient,

00:07:38.516 --> 00:07:39.653
you'll need this.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.385
- Get this thing out of my sight!

00:07:43.385 --> 00:07:45.160
- Wait, is that not even him?

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.450
Is that someone doing
his voice really well?

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.090
- Wait a second, how you
gonna play by the rules,

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.680
if you don't have the rule book, Oh!

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.707
- My methods are a little
unorthodox, Mr. Griffin.

00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:55.852
But I think I can help.

00:07:55.852 --> 00:07:56.685
(Dr. House punches the patient)

00:07:56.685 --> 00:07:57.518
- Oh!

00:07:57.518 --> 00:07:58.351
- What are you doing?

00:07:58.351 --> 00:07:59.184
- My job.

00:07:59.184 --> 00:08:00.145
Sometimes people fake being in a coma,

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:01.894
this man's not unless of course,

00:08:01.894 --> 00:08:03.419
the first punch knocked him out.

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:04.480
(laughs)

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.307
- He literally is the type
of doctor that's like,

00:08:06.307 --> 00:08:07.660
"All right, I think it could be this.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.020
Let's poison the patient
and see if they recover."

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.030
And I'm like, that's not really
how you practice medicine.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:14.110
- Sir, I think you should know

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:17.412
these growths on your
forearms, they're giant tumors.

00:08:17.412 --> 00:08:18.730
(Popeye speaks gibberish)

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.440
- I just realized Popeye
was not a healthy character.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.140
Why are the forearms
huge, and the biceps tiny?

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:27.170
And then second of all,
what's with the pipe?

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:29.290
Is he always smoking
cause that's not healthy,

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:30.800
no matter how much spinach you eat.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.735
- And the speech thing and what
